package docs

// ─── Memory & Artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsMemoryArtifacts() {
	@docsSection("memory", "Memory & Artifacts", iconBrain, "text-cyan-500") {
		<h3>Cross-Session Memory</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Cross-session memory</strong> is a built-in agent capability that gives
			each agent persistent recall across conversations. When enabled, sessions are
			indexed after each run for fast keyword search, and the agent gets two
			complementary recall paths:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Automatic preload</strong> — before every turn, the agent searches past
				conversations using the user's current message and injects matching context into
				its instructions. The agent doesn't need to do anything — memory just works.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>On-demand recall</strong> — the agent can also search memory explicitly
				with a custom query, useful when it needs to look up something specific that
				differs from the user's current message (e.g. "What did we discuss about the
				API redesign last week?").
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Memory is an <strong>agent feature</strong>, not a tool or plugin — toggle it per
			agent in the designer's Memory section with two switches:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Remember past conversations</strong> — turns memory on for this agent.
				Adds an on-demand search tool so the model can look things up in prior sessions
				when it decides it needs to.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Auto-recall every turn</strong> — also searches memory before each reply
				and injects relevant context automatically. Costs an extra search per turn;
				useful for agents that should feel like they already remember, without the model
				having to decide to look.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h4>What to expect</h4>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Matches on words, not meaning.</strong>
				"colour" and "colours" both match; "colour" and "hue" don't. Phrase things the
				way you'd expect the agent to encounter them again.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Your memory is yours.</strong>
				An agent only sees your past conversations — never another user's.
				Everything you've said to any of your agents pools into one searchable history.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>A conversation becomes searchable as each reply completes.</strong>
				Everything in the current conversation is already in the agent's context;
				content from other conversations becomes recallable once a turn in them
				has finished.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Code Execution</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Code execution</strong> lets an agent run Python to do things it would
			otherwise guess at — multi-step math, data manipulation, unit conversions,
			parsing, format normalisation. When enabled, the agent can write code, run it,
			read the output, and use the result to form its final reply.
		</p>
		<p>
			Code runs in the AI provider's own sandbox, never on our servers. It's
			isolated, has no network access, and keeps no state between turns. The chat
			shows a read-only card with the source and output so you can audit exactly
			what ran.
		</p>
		<h4>What to expect</h4>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Best for precise work.</strong> Give the agent code execution when
				you'd rather it compute than guess — arithmetic on long numbers, date maths,
				regex, JSON reshaping, statistics.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>No network, no file persistence.</strong> The sandbox can't call
				external APIs or remember things between turns. If the agent needs data from
				the internet, pair it with a web-search tool; if it needs persistent files,
				enable Artifacts.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Available on Gemini and OpenAI today.</strong> The toggle saves on
				any provider, but only those two actually run the code right now. A warning
				appears in the designer when the selected provider doesn't support it yet.
				Support for other providers will come incrementally without any change to
				the toggle.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Versioned Artifacts</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Versioned artifacts</strong> are a built-in agent capability for creating,
			saving, and loading persistent files across sessions — reports, CSVs, generated
			images, refined code. Each save creates a new version, and the agent always works
			with the latest version of each file; older versions are retained.
		</p>
		<p>
			Like memory, artifacts are an <strong>agent feature</strong>, not a tool or plugin
			— toggle them per agent in the designer with two switches:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Use artifacts</strong> — turns the capability on. The agent can list
				available artifacts and load them into the conversation; new versions are
				written by capabilities that produce files (document generation,
				text-to-speech, auto-saved uploads).
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Auto-save uploaded files</strong> — additionally saves files you attach
				in chat as artifacts automatically, handing the agent a reference to the file
				so it can refer back to it later.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Use cases include:</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>• Reports and analysis documents that evolve across sessions</li>
			<li>• CSV/data exports from data processing agents</li>
			<li>• Generated images and visualisations</li>
			<li>• Code files and scripts refined iteratively</li>
			<li>• User-scoped files shared across all sessions (prefix with <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted text-sm font-mono">user:</code>)</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			The chat UI shows an <strong>Artifact Version Saved</strong> card whenever an agent
			creates or updates a file, showing the file name and its new version number. Open
			the Artifacts panel in chat to see every file the conversation has produced and
			download any of them.
		</p>
		<h3>Thread Management</h3>
		<p>
			All conversations are organized into <strong>threads</strong>. You can:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>• Create new threads for different topics</li>
			<li>• Rename threads for easy identification</li>
			<li>• Search across all conversations</li>
			<li>• Delete threads when no longer needed</li>
			<li>• View full message history with tool usage details</li>
		</ul>
	}
}

// ─── File Attachments ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsFileAttachments() {
	@docsSection("file-attachments", "File Attachments", iconPaperclip, "text-rose-500") {
		<p>
			Compass.AI supports <strong>file attachments in chat messages</strong>, allowing you to send images,
			audio files, PDFs, and other documents directly to your AI agents for analysis and discussion.
		</p>
		<h3>Supported File Types</h3>
		@docsTable() {
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Category</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Formats</th>
					<th class="text-start py-2 px-3 text-sm font-medium border-b border-border">Use Case</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				@docsTableRow("Images", "PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP", "Visual analysis, diagram review, photo questions")
				@docsTableRow("Audio", "MP3, WAV, OGG, WebM", "Transcription, audio analysis")
				@docsTableRow("Documents", "PDF, TXT, Markdown, CSV", "Document review, content extraction")
			</tbody>
		}
		<h3>How Attachments Work</h3>
		<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>Click the attachment icon in the chat input bar</li>
			<li>Select one or more files from your device</li>
			<li>Files are securely uploaded and attached to your message</li>
			<li>The AI agent receives the file content alongside your text message</li>
			<li>The agent can analyze, summarize, or discuss the attached content</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			You can also attach files by pasting them from the clipboard or dragging and
			dropping them onto the chat input.
		</p>
		<p>
			Attachments are stored per-thread and persist with the conversation history. The maximum
			upload size is <strong>20 MB per file</strong>, with up to <strong>5 files per message</strong>.
		</p>
	}
}

// ─── Conversation Search ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
templ docsConversationSearch() {
	@docsSection("conversation-search", "Conversation Search", iconSearch, "text-sky-500") {
		<p>
			Compass.AI lets you <strong>quickly search your conversations by title</strong>, making it easy
			to find past discussions.
		</p>
		<h3>Search Capabilities</h3>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Thread Search</strong> -- Search across all your thread titles
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Quick Navigation</strong> -- Click any search result to jump directly to the thread
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Thread Management</h3>
		<p>
			All conversations are organized into <strong>threads</strong>. Each thread has:
		</p>
		<ul class="space-y-2 my-4">
			<li>
				<strong>Auto-generated titles</strong> -- Compass.AI automatically titles threads based on the conversation content
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Rename</strong> -- Customize thread titles for easy identification
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Full history</strong> -- Every message, tool call, and artifact is preserved
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Delete</strong> -- Remove threads when they are no longer needed
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Your full conversation history is stored reliably and loads quickly, even for long threads.
		</p>
	}
}
